#ddev-generated
# Build toolchain for the theme asset pipeline.
#
# rdg-webpack pulls in imagemin, whose binary dependencies (gifsicle, optipng, mozjpeg)
# ship x86_64-only prebuilts. On Apple silicon yarn therefore compiles them from source
# during `yarn install`, which needs autotools. Without them the theme daemon crash-loops
# on "theme-watch: dependency install failed", preceded by the real cause a few lines
# earlier -- either "Command failed: .../gifsicle/vendor/gifsicle --version" (the prebuilt
# will not run) or "Command failed: /bin/sh -c autoreconf -ivf" (it fell back to building
# and the tools are missing). Upsun's composable image has this toolchain and
# ddev-webserver does not, which is why the pipeline works in production either way.
#
# Shipped unconditionally, for every repo the add-on installs into, rather than generated
# by `ddev rdg-sync` for the ones whose package.json declares a theme build. Three reasons:
#
#  - It is identical everywhere, and was previously maintained three ways: generated on
#    Upsun repos, hand-written on the others.
#  - Deriving it needs Upsun config, so native repos could never get it from the add-on
#    at all -- they each kept their own copy of these three lines.
#  - The cost of shipping it to a repo with no theme is a few apt packages at image-build
#    time. That is why this can be unconditional where a docker-compose.*.yaml cannot:
#    DDEV activates every compose file, and a container costs memory and ports for as
#    long as the project runs.
#
# A Dockerfile rather than webimage_extra_packages, for isolation. DDEV *appends* list
# keys when merging config.*.yaml, so writing that key would not discard packages a repo
# set for its own reasons -- but DDEV reads every web-build/Dockerfile.*, which keeps an
# add-on-owned file out of a key the repo also edits.
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
    autoconf automake libtool dh-autoreconf make pkg-config zlib1g-dev \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
